# Verification-only parent/child loader separation

The previous generated experiment is immutable. Its simple-actions source parsed,
linked and validated, generated and compiled, but all nine runtime processes
failed before JSON with a signed-package SecurityException and consequential
Guice missing binding. These are workflow failures, not model rejections.

This addendum changes only the host class-loading boundary. The ordinary system
loader loads the sixty byte-pinned dependency JARs, preserving JAR verification,
signers and certificates. A child owns exactly the freshly generated and compiled
classes plus raw grammar/token/properties resources listed in a frozen whitelist.
Each unsigned generated package is defined only in that child; signed support
classes remain in the parent. The exact generated Terminals classes and grammar
resources are retained; no cached built-in grammar replaces the authored library.
DefaultTerminalConverters must remain a signed parent-loaded dependency.

The whitelist rejects digest mismatch, missing or extra owned files, ambiguous
resource names, duplicate roots/JARs, path escape and a changed parent classpath.
Only the original NodeSnapshotRuntime main can run. Its helper bytecode, authored
URI bindings, provider, grammar, text and named-entry arguments stay unchanged.
The thread context loader is restored on success or failure. Source, runtime
grammar URI, diagnostics, model and node observations receive no normalization.

Use both independently generated previous record/replay class trees. The eight
successful grouped-companion runtime observations serve as an exact complete-JSON
control; all nine simple-actions requests remain visible beside their original
no-JSON failures. Capture new record/fresh-JVM replay separately and preserve all
failure stages. Negative whitelist experiments alter new specifications only.
No JAR is copied, stripped, rewritten or redefined. No Java enters production.
This is verification workflow evidence, not native admission or a gate closure.
